We want to find the angle between two planes, one of which is $x+3y+2z=0$, and the other which has normal vector $(1,-2,-1)$.  To do this, we use the dot product formula for angles:  If vectors $\mathbf{a}$ and $\mathbf{b}$ have lengths $|\mathbf{a}|$ and $|\mathbf{b}|$, respectively, then their cosine of the angle between them is given by:
\[\cos{\theta}=\frac{\mathbf{a}\cdot\mathbf{b}}{|\mathbf{a}||\mathbf{b}|}.\]

Here, $\mathbf{a}$ is the vector formed by the point $(1/6,-1/4,-1/4)$ on plane $L_2$ and the origin; similarly, $\mathbf{b}$ is the vector formed by the point $(-3/2, -2/3, 1)$ on plane $L_1$ and the origin.  Thus,
\begin{align*}
\mathbf{a}&=(1/6, -1/4, -1/4),\\
\mathbf{b}&=(-3/2, -2/3, 1).
\end{align*}

Then
\begin{align*}
\mathbf{a}\cdot\mathbf{b}&=\left(\frac{1}{6},-\frac{1}{4},-\frac{1}{4}\right)\cdot\left(-\frac{3}{2},-\frac{2}{3},1\right)=\frac{-1}{6}-\frac{1}{8}+\frac{1}{4}=-\frac{1}{9}.
\end{align*}

Also,
\begin{align*}
|\mathbf{a}|&=\sqrt{\left(\frac{1}{6}\right)^2 + \left(-\frac{1}{4}\right)^2 + \left(-\frac{1}{4}\right)^2}=\sqrt{\frac{1}{36}+\frac{1}{16}+\frac{1}{16}}=\sqrt{\frac{7}{72}},\\
|\mathbf{b}|&=\sqrt{\left(-\frac{3}{2}\right)^2 + \left(-\frac{2}{3}\right)^2 + 1^2}=\sqrt{\frac{9}{4}+\frac{4}{9}+1}=\sqrt{\frac{65}{36}}.
\end{align*}

Thus,
\begin{align*}
\cos{\theta}&=\frac{\mathbf{a}\cdot\mathbf{b}}{|\mathbf{a}||\mathbf{b}|}=\frac{-\frac{1}{9}}{\sqrt{\frac{7}{72}}\sqrt{\frac{65}{36}}}=\frac{-\frac{1}{9}}{\frac{\sqrt{7}}{6}\sqrt{\frac{65}{36}}}=-\frac{1}{3},
\end{align*}

so $\theta=120^\circ.$

