We know that we can write $$f(x)=(x^2-1)q(x)+ax+b$$ for some constants $a,b\in\mathbb{R}$ and polynomial function $q(x)\in\mathbb{R}[x]$. Now, to determine these two unknowns, we will use the fact that the remainder of dividing any polynomial by another polynomial must have degree less than the divisor's. Therefore, if we set $x=1$, then $(1^2-1)q(1)=a+b=f(1)$ which gives us an equation involving only one variable, namely $a+b$. Similarly, if we set $x=-1$, then $((-1)^2-1)q(-1)=a-b=f(-1)$ which again gives us an equation involving only one variable, namely $a-b$. From here we can solve this system of equations for $a$ and $b$, giving us our desired remainder as $f(x)$ modulo $x^2-1$. Note that since $f(1)=f(-1)$, we get rid of one of the variables immediately! So let's begin.
\begin{align*}
a+b&=f(1)\\
a-b&=f(-1)
\end{align*}
Adding these together yields $2a=b+f(1)-f(-1)$, so solving for $a$ gives $a=\frac{b+f(1)-f(-1)}{2}$. Substituting into the second equation, we have $b-a=f(-1)-f(1)$ or $b-\frac{b+f(1)-f(-1)}{2}=f(-1)-f(1)$. Solving for $b$ and substituting back, we get $b=\frac{2f(-1)-(f(-1)-f(1))}{3}=\frac{f(-1)+f(1)}{3}$. Finally, plugging everything back in to our original expression, we see that $\boxed{\frac{x^2+x-2}{3}}$ is the remainder when $f(x)$ is divided by $x^2-1$.

