We can use Pick's Theorem to find the area of this octagon.  Note that we cannot just count the lattice points around the polygon since there are some inside it (in fact there are 8 such points).  We can however calculate the total number of lattice points as follows:  Since each side has an integer number of lattice points along its entire length, the sum of these numbers must be even.  Thus, if we subtract the interior lattice points from this sum, the result will also be even.  So the difference between the number of lattice points on all the edges and the number of lattice points inside the polygon is always even.  Therefore, our calculation for the area must be an integer!  Let us now compute the perimeter of the octagon:

