\[x^2 - x - 6 > 0,\]
which factors as $(x + 2)(x - 3) > 0$. This occurs when $x < -2$ or $x > 3$, so we can write
\[x^2 - x - 6 = (x + 2)(x - 3) > 0.\]
Next, let's find when the right-hand side of our inequality is greater than zero.
\[2x - 3 > 0,\]
so $x > \frac{3}{2}$.

Now we want to solve the inequality
\[(x + 2)(x - 3) < 4x^2 - 12x + 9,\]
or equivalently,
\[4x^2 - 14x + 15 < 0.\]
The quadratic equation $4x^2 - 14x + 15 = 0$ has roots $\frac{7 \pm i\sqrt{3}}{4}$, which lie outside the complex plane and therefore do not affect this inequality. The graph of $y = 4x^2 - 14x + 15$ is shown below:

